[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Authentication and Authorization in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Security is a foundational aspect of modern web applications. In the .NET ecosystem, securing APIs and web applications involves understanding a robust suite of protocols and concepts: Authentication, Authorization, JSON Web Tokens (JWT), OAuth2, OpenID Connect (OIDC), and Policy-Based Authorization."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts Defined"},{"order":4,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authentication (AuthN): The process of verifying who a user is (e.g., logging in with credentials)."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Authorization (AuthZ): Determining what an authenticated user is allowed to do."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OAuth2: An authorization framework that enables applications to obtain limited access to user accounts on an HTTP service."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"OpenID Connect (OIDC): An identity layer on top of OAuth2 that provides user authentication and profile information."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"JWT: A compact, URL-safe means of representing claims to be transferred between two parties."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring JWT Authentication in .NET"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"To secure a .NET Web API using JWT Bearer authentication, you first need to install the Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer NuGet package. Then, configure the services in Program.cs as shown below."},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddAuthentication(JwtBearerDefaults.AuthenticationScheme)
    .AddJwtBearer(options =>
    {
        options.TokenValidationParameters = new TokenValidationParameters
        {
            ValidateIssuer = true,
            ValidateAudience = true,
            ValidateLifetime = true,
            ValidateIssuerSigningKey = true,
            ValidIssuer = builder.Configuration["Jwt:Issuer"],
            ValidAudience = builder.Configuration["Jwt:Audience"],
            IssuerSigningKey = new SymmetricSecurityKey(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(builder.Configuration["Jwt:Key"]!))
        };
    });

builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
var app = builder.Build();

app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Implementing Policy-Based Authorization"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Role-based authorization is often insufficient for complex business logic. .NET provides Policy-Based Authorization, allowing you to evaluate rules, claims, and requirements against the user context."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"builder.Services.AddAuthorization(options =>
{
    options.AddPolicy("AtLeast21", policy =>
        policy.RequireAssertion(context =>
            context.User.HasClaim(c => c.Type == "DateOfBirth") &&
            CalculateAge(DateTime.Parse(context.User.FindFirst(c => c.Type == "DateOfBirth")!.Value)) >= 21));
});"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Applying Policies to Endpoints"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a policy is registered, you can enforce it on controllers or Minimal APIs using the Authorize attribute or extension methods."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"app.MapGet("/admin/dashboard", () => "Welcome to the secured dashboard!")
   .RequireAuthorization("AtLeast21");"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"By combining JWT authentication with robust OpenID Connect providers and fine-grained Policy-Based Authorization, .NET developers can build secure, scalable, and enterprise-ready distributed applications."}]
